---
/**
 * Privacy policy for soot and The Wired's servers.
 *
 * DRAFT — not legal advice. Counsel must review before publication, and the
 * bracketed placeholders must be filled: the operator name and jurisdiction
 * come from the app's legal text via legal.json; [log retention] must match
 * the servers' log rotation.
 *
 * Every claim here describes what the code actually stores — keep it in step
 * with services/backend (schema + workers) and services/relay when they change.
 */
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import legal from "../data/legal.json";

const UPDATED = "2026-10-01";
const operator = legal.operator.name;
const contact = Object.fromEntries(legal.contacts.map((c) => [c.kind, c.address]));
---

<LegalLayout
  title="privacy policy"
  description="What soot and The Wired's servers keep about you, who else sees it, and how to delete it."
  path="/privacy"
  updated={UPDATED}
>
  <section>
    <h2>who we are</h2>
    <p>
      {operator} ("we") runs the soot app's default servers at thewired.app: a Nostr relay, an API, a search
      index, a media store for uploads, and a voice server. This policy covers those servers and the app. It does
      not cover relays or services run by other people, even when soot connects to them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>your account is a key pair</h2>
    <p>
      There is no email, phone number or password. Your account is a key pair created on your device; the public
      half identifies you, and the secret half never leaves your device (or the signer you chose). We cannot see it,
      reset it or recover it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>what our servers keep</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your public key</strong>, which ties everything below to your account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A copy of your public profile</strong> (name, picture, bio, verified handle, Lightning address,
        website), so names and pictures load quickly. It is the same profile you publish to everyone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What you publish to our relay</strong>: posts, replies, reactions, space chat, articles, music
        releases and playlists. Public posts are public. Posts in a space are served only to its members.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Direct messages, encrypted.</strong> Messages are end-to-end encrypted on your device before they are
        sent. Our relay stores only the encrypted envelope and can see whose inbox it is for — that is how we send a
        "new message" notification, which carries no sender and no text. We cannot read your messages.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Push notification details</strong>: your device's push token, bound to your public key, with the
        platform, app version and language, and your notification settings (muted spaces, people you chose to be
        alerted about). Devices that have not been seen for 90 days are removed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spaces</strong>: which spaces you belong to, your roles there, invites you created and answers you gave
        to a space's onboarding questions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Uploads</strong>: the audio, artwork and other files you upload, with their size, type, a content hash,
        the upload time and the account that uploaded them, plus the streaming versions we make of your audio.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Plays</strong>: when you play a track we count it and remember that you listened (per track, with the
        last time and a count), and keep your last 500 plays to build recommendations.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Searches</strong>: your search terms are sent to our search index to answer you and, like every
        request, appear in our server logs. GIF searches are passed on to our GIF provider (Klipy) by our server,
        so Klipy sees the search terms but not your device or account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reports</strong>: when you report something we keep your public key (or, if you were not signed in, a
        one-way hash of your IP address), what you reported (by id — never the text of a message), the reason, your
        note, and what we decided.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Server logs</strong>: request paths and times, and the IP address of each connection to our relay,
        for security and abuse prevention. They are kept for [log retention].
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Voice and video</strong>: calls and voice rooms pass through our voice server end-to-end encrypted. We
        see who is in a room while it is open; we do not record or store calls.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>what goes beyond our servers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Other relays.</strong> Nostr is an open network. What you post is also sent to other public relays,
        and anyone can copy it from there. We do not run those relays and cannot delete what they hold.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Push delivery.</strong> Notifications reach your phone through Expo's push service and Apple's or
        Google's. They process the token and the notification text; message notifications carry no content.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The assistant.</strong> Navi talks to the AI provider you choose, with your own key, directly from your
        device under that provider's terms. Nothing you send to Navi passes through our servers, and soot asks before
        anything is sent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tips.</strong> Zaps go from your Lightning wallet to the recipient's. We never hold funds or see your
        wallet's balance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Email.</strong> Mail you send to our addresses is forwarded through Cloudflare's email routing.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>We do not sell your data, show ads, or use third-party analytics or tracking.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>how long we keep it</h2>
    <p>
      We keep data for as long as your account exists, except where noted above. Our databases are backed up daily,
      and backups age out on a rotation of 7 daily, 4 weekly and 3 monthly copies, so deleted data can survive in a
      backup for up to about three months before it is gone for good.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>deleting your account</h2>
    <p>
      In soot, go to settings, then account &amp; security, and delete your account. Our servers then remove your push
      details and notification settings, your profile copy and verified handle, your space memberships, your music,
      uploads and play history, and everything you published to our relay, including messages addressed to you.
      Spaces you created are deleted or handed to their remaining moderators, as you choose. The app also asks every
      other relay to erase what you published — a request they may or may not honour — and removes your key and
      data from the device.
    </p>
    <p>
      We keep a record that you asked (the signed request), moderation records such as reports and bans in spaces
      that still exist, and reports you filed, with your key removed from them.
    </p>
    <p>
      For anything else — a copy of what we hold, a correction, or a question about this policy — write to{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${contact.privacy}`}>{contact.privacy}</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>children</h2>
    <p>soot is not for anyone under {legal.operator.minimumAge}.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>changes and contact</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes in a way that matters, we will say so in the app. Privacy:{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${contact.privacy}`}>{contact.privacy}</a>. Everything else:{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${contact.support}`}>{contact.support}</a>. This policy is governed by the law of{" "}
      {legal.operator.jurisdiction}.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalLayout>
